  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 212 AM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nHigh pressure continues to settle into the mid-Mississippi \nValley and will remain in place through Tuesday. As a result, \ndry conditions with generally comfortable humidity are expected \nto start off the work week. Temperatures today will once again \nbe on the seasonably cool side with highs only topping out in \nthe upper 70s to around 80 this afternoon. A lake breeze is \nexpected to develop and move inland this afternoon and evening \nwhich will result in cooling temperatures along the lakeshore, \nthough with lake temperatures in the lower 70s the temperature \ndrop shouldn't be overly impressive. Overnight lows tonight \nwon't be as chilly as they will be this morning, but will dip \ninto the mid-50s inland with upper 50s near the lake. As winds \nturn more south-southwesterly on Tuesday, warmer air should \nbegin to advect into the area and result with more seasonable \nhighs in the lower 80s. \n  \nRain chances are still forecast to increase as we head into the\nmiddle of the week as the shortwave trough over the Mountain \nWest pivots into the Great Lakes. A cold front is expected to \naccompany the shortwave as it moves through the region which \nshould serve as the impetus for showers and thunderstorms. \nSimilar to last night, guidance continues to show some \nshowers/storms developing along the front in the Upper Midwest \non Tuesday and drifting towards northern IL Tuesday night. \nThough, with moisture return continuing to look more limited \nlocally and the unfavorable diurnal time, these showers/storms \nshould be in a decaying state as they approach IL (if they make \nit at) so the greatest coverage of any rain Tuesday night should\nbe near the IL-WI line. The front is expected to continue \nmoving across northern IL and northwest IN on Wednesday which \nshould allow for the redevelopment of showers and thunderstorms \nWednesday afternoon and evening as diurnal destabilization \noccurs. Despite the decent MLCAPE values noted in forecast \nsoundings, shower and storm coverage continues to look spotty \nlikely due to weaker frontal convergence and the core of the \nshortwave currently forecast to be centered over MI. So while \nthis may mean some areas get little rain, our official forecast \nwill continue to advertise broad 30 to locally 50% chances for \nshowers and storms on Wednesday. Regardless of storm coverage, \ndeep layer shear values of 25-35 kts are still expected in the \narea which may support a few storms becoming strong to locally \nsevere Wednesday afternoon and evening. With confidence on storm\ncoverage being low, our area has not been added to any severe \noutlooks at this time, but we will continue to keep an eye on \nthis potential. \n\nThe cold front should exit the area late Wednesday night into \nThursday morning which looks to allow another surface high to \nbuild into the area. However, with some subtle frontal timing \ndifferences noted in ensemble guidance the exact time the front \nexits remains uncertain. Nevertheless, the building high \npressure behind the front will result in a period of dry weather\nto close out the work week with otherwise seasonable \ntemperatures expected. The signal remains for a shift towards a \nmore active upper-level pattern as we head towards the beginning\nof next week which looks to mean more shower and thunderstorm \nchances so enjoy the nice weather while we have it.\n\nYack",
